+\subsection{Research Track: Transverse-projector response, helicity constraint,
+and finite-core electromagnetic matching}
+\label{subsec:rt_alpha_transverse_response_v0835}
+
+\subsubsection{Status and logical separation}
+
+\textbf{[ORTHODOX INPUT / DERIVED KINEMATIC IDENTITY / RESEARCH MATCHING
+PROGRAMME].}
+This module separates four claims that must not be collapsed:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item the isotropic incompressible transverse projector gives the exact
+    factor \(8\pi/3\);
+    \item the bare projector and constant circular tube volume give no
+    geometry-sensitive correction;
+    \item Euler--Biot--Savart dynamics permits a finite-core expansion in
+    curvature, material twist, and contact operators;
+    \item identification of that response with
+    \(\alpha_{\rm eff}^{-1}\) requires the independent gauge-emergence
+    certificate of
+    Subsection~\ref{sec:rt_gauge_emergence_certification_ladder}.
+\end{enumerate}
+Only the first statement is presently closed without additional microscopic
+input.
+
+\subsubsection{Isotropic transverse-projector identity}
+
+For a unit wavevector \(\widehat{\mathbf k}\in S^2\), define
+\begin{align}
+    P_{ij}(\widehat{\mathbf k})
+    &:=
+    \delta_{ij}-\widehat k_i\widehat k_j.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_transverse_projector_v0835}
+\end{align}
+For a unit centreline tangent \(\mathbf t\),
+\begin{align}
+    t_iP_{ij}t_j
+    =
+    1-(\mathbf t\cdot\widehat{\mathbf k})^2.
+\end{align}
+Rotational invariance fixes the second moment
+\begin{align}
+    \int_{S^2}
+    \widehat k_i\widehat k_j\,d\Omega
+    =
+    \frac{4\pi}{3}\delta_{ij}.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_isotropic_second_moment_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \int_{S^2}t_iP_{ij}t_j\,d\Omega
+    &=
+    4\pi
+    -
+    t_it_j\frac{4\pi}{3}\delta_{ij}
+    \nonumber\\
+    &=
+    4\pi-\frac{4\pi}{3}|\mathbf t|^2
+    =
+    \boxed{\frac{8\pi}{3}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_projector_identity_v0835}
+\end{align}
+The denominator \(3\) is the isotropic second-moment denominator in three
+spatial dimensions.  It is not generated by the trefoil crossing number,
+threefold visual symmetry, or an observed nine-step contact orbit.
+
+With \(\sigma=s/D\) and
+\(\mathcal L_D=L/D\), the leading local response is
+\begin{align}
+    \mathscr R_0[\gamma]
+    &:=
+    \int_\gamma d\sigma
+    \int_{S^2}t_iP_{ij}t_j\,d\Omega
+    =
+    \frac{8\pi}{3}\mathcal L_D.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_leading_response_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Since the angular integral is the same at every arclength point,
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{\Delta_{\rm local\ projector}=0}.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_local_projector_no_go_v0835}
+\end{align}
+A nonzero correction must modify the response kernel or introduce finite-core,
+material-frame, or nonlocal data.
+
+\subsubsection{Radius--diameter convention and tube-volume route}
+
+Let \(a=D/2\) and define the standard radius-normalized ropelength
+\(\mathcal R_a=L/a\).  Then
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal R_a
+    =
+    2\mathcal L_D,
+    \qquad
+    \mathscr R_0
+    =
+    \frac{4\pi}{3}\mathcal R_a
+    =
+    \frac{8\pi}{3}\mathcal L_D.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_radius_diameter_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Thus trefoil values near \(32.74\) from Ridgerunner, KnotPlot-derived
+polygons, Fresnel/Fourier series, or radius-thickness literature are
+compatible with diameter-normalized values near \(16.37\).  They are not a
+second branch of the response.
+
+For a constant circular tube before volume overlap,
+\begin{align}
+    V_{\rm tube}
+    =
+    \pi a^2L,
+\end{align}
+so
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{4}{3}\frac{V_{\rm tube}}{a^3}
+    =
+    \frac{4\pi L}{3a}
+    =
+    \frac{8\pi}{3}\mathcal L_D.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_tube_volume_identity_v0835}
+\end{align}
+This is an exact geometric rewriting, not an electromagnetic derivation.
+In Frenet tube coordinates,
+\begin{align}
+    dV
+    =
+    r\left(1-\kappa r\cos\phi\right)
+    dr\,d\phi\,ds.
+\end{align}
+The term linear in \(\kappa\) integrates to zero over \(\phi\).  Therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{\Delta_{\rm constant\ tube\ volume}=0}.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_tube_volume_no_go_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Core deformation, nonuniform profile, contact overlap, or dynamical stress is
+required for a geometry-sensitive tube correction.
+
+\subsubsection{Euler energy and regularized vortex response}
+
+For an incompressible velocity field \(\mathbf u\), with
+\(\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u\), the kinetic energy is
+\begin{align}
+    E_{\rm Euler}
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoF}{2}
+    \int_{\Omega}|\mathbf u(\mathbf x)|^2\,d^3x.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_euler_energy_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Under the bounded-domain admissibility conditions of
+Subsection~\ref{subsec:bounded_domain_biot_savart}, or under the standard
+whole-space decay assumptions, the corresponding vorticity form is
+\begin{align}
+    E_{\rm Euler}
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoF}{8\pi}
+    \int d^3x\,d^3x'\,
+    \frac{
+        \boldsymbol\omega(\mathbf x)\cdot
+        \boldsymbol\omega(\mathbf x')
+    }{
+        |\mathbf x-\mathbf x'|
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_vorticity_energy_v0835}
+\end{align}
+For one formal zero-radius filament,
+\begin{align}
+    \boldsymbol\omega(\mathbf x)
+    =
+    \Gamma
+    \oint_\gamma
+    \mathbf t(s)
+    \delta^{(3)}
+    \!\left(\mathbf x-\boldsymbol\gamma(s)\right)ds,
+\end{align}
+which gives
+\begin{align}
+    E_{\rm BS}[\gamma]
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoF\Gamma^2}{8\pi}
+    \oint_\gamma\oint_\gamma
+    \frac{
+        \mathbf t(s)\cdot\mathbf t(s')
+    }{
+        |\boldsymbol\gamma(s)-\boldsymbol\gamma(s')|
+    }
+    ds\,ds'.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_filament_biot_savart_energy_v0835}
+\end{align}
+The diagonal divergence is not a physical SST result.  It must be replaced by
+a declared finite-core profile and regulator
+\cite{Saffman1992,HornNicolisPenco2015}.
+
+With \(\mathbf X=\boldsymbol\gamma/D\), define the dimensionless regularized
+energy
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal E_f[\gamma]
+    &:=
+    \frac{8\pi E_f}{\rhoF\Gamma^2D}
+    \nonumber\\
+    &=
+    \operatorname{FP}
+    \oint d\sigma\,d\sigma'\,
+    \mathbf t(\sigma)\cdot\mathbf t(\sigma')
+    G_f\!\left(
+        |\mathbf X(\sigma)-\mathbf X(\sigma')|
+    \right),
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_regularized_vortex_energy_v0835}
+\end{align}
+where \(G_f\) is fixed by the core profile and
+\(\operatorname{FP}\) denotes the declared subtraction of the local self-part.
+A low-order parity-even response expansion is
+\begin{align}
+    \mathscr R_{\rm SST}
+    &=
+    \frac{8\pi}{3}\mathcal L_D
+    +
+    \Delta_{\rm micro}^{(+)},
+    \\
+    \Delta_{\rm micro}^{(+)}
+    &=
+    c_\kappa\mathcal I_{\kappa^2}
+    +
+    c_\Omega\mathcal I_{\Omega^2}
+    +
+    c_C\mathcal C_{\rm contact}
+    +
+    O\!\left(
+        \widehat\kappa^4,
+        \partial_\sigma^2\widehat\kappa,
+        \widehat\kappa^2\widehat\Omega^2
+    \right),
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_finite_core_response_v0835}
+\end{align}
+with
+\begin{align}
+    \widehat\kappa&:=D\kappa,
+    &
+    \widehat\Omega&:=D\Omega_3,
+    \\
+    \mathcal I_{\kappa^2}
+    &:=
+    \int_\gamma\widehat\kappa^2\,d\sigma,
+    &
+    \mathcal I_{\Omega^2}
+    &:=
+    \int_\gamma\widehat\Omega^2\,d\sigma.
+\end{align}
+The length counterterm is fixed by
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{c_L=0}.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_length_renormalization_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Otherwise \(c_L\mathcal L_D\) is indistinguishable from changing the leading
+coefficient \(8\pi/3\) after the target is known.
+
+\subsubsection{Helicity as a twist-energy constraint}
+
+The existing thin-tube relation
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{\mathcal H}{\Gamma^2}
+    \simeq
+    Wr+Tw
+\end{align}
+is recorded in Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_helicity_wr_tw_sector}.  For one framed tube
+without reconnection, write
+\begin{align}
+    SL
+    =
+    Wr+Tw,
+    \qquad
+    Tw
+    =
+    \frac{1}{2\pi}
+    \oint_\gamma\Omega_3\,ds.
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_selflink_twist_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Here \(\Omega_3\) is material-frame twist.  It is not generally equal to
+unregularized Frenet torsion.
+
+Cauchy--Schwarz gives
+\begin{align}
+    \left(
+        \int_\gamma D\Omega_3\,\frac{ds}{D}
+    \right)^2
+    &\leq
+    \left(
+        \int_\gamma\frac{ds}{D}
+    \right)
+    \left(
+        \int_\gamma(D\Omega_3)^2\frac{ds}{D}
+    \right).
+\end{align}
+Therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \mathcal I_{\Omega^2}
+    \geq
+    \frac{4\pi^2}{\mathcal L_D}
+    (SL-Wr)^2.
+    }
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_twist_energy_bound_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Equality holds only for uniform material twist.  Helicity therefore fixes a
+topological sector and constrains the minimum parity-even twist energy; it
+does not determine \(c_\Omega\).
+
+Since \(\mathcal H/\Gamma^2\), \(Wr\), and \(Tw\) are pseudoscalars, the linear
+ansatz
+\begin{align}
+    \Delta_{\mathcal H}^{(-)}
+    =
+    c_H\frac{\mathcal H}{\Gamma^2}
+\end{align}
+is parity odd.  It belongs, if independently derived, to the separate
+parity-odd response channel guarded by
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_parity_odd_response_term}, with a possible infrared image
+proportional to \(f_{\mu\nu}\widetilde f^{\mu\nu}\).  It is not part of the
+ordinary parity-even Maxwell stiffness.
+
+Under the additional uniform-twist ansatz, the parity-even completion becomes
+\begin{align}
+    \Delta_{\rm micro}^{(+)}
+    &=
+    c_\kappa\mathcal I_{\kappa^2}
+    +
+    c_\Omega
+    \frac{4\pi^2}{\mathcal L_D}
+    (SL-Wr)^2
+    +
+    c_C\mathcal C_{\rm contact}
+    +\cdots .
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_uniform_twist_completion_v0835}
+\end{align}
+This is a falsifiable reduction of the twist operator, not a topological
+prediction of its coefficient.
+
+\subsubsection{Electromagnetic gauge-stiffness matching}
+
+The transverse projector counts two transverse spatial components but does
+not by itself derive Maxwell electrodynamics.  Let \(a_\mu\) be a
+dimensionless infrared candidate field and
+\(f_{\mu\nu}:=\partial_\mu a_\nu-\partial_\nu a_\mu\).  The matching target is
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{S_{\rm SST,IR}^{(2)}}{\hbar}
+    =
+    -\frac{\mathscr R_{\rm SST}}{16\pi}
+    \int d^4\xi\,f_{\mu\nu}f^{\mu\nu}
+    +
+    \int d^4\xi\,j^\mu a_\mu
+    +
+    O(\partial^4,f^3).
+    \label{eq:rt_alpha_em_action_v0835}
+\end{align}
+Comparison with the rescaled gauge action
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{S_{\rm gauge}^{(2)}}{\hbar}
+    =
+    -\frac{1}{4g_{\rm eff}^{\,2}}
+    \int d^4\xi\,f_{\mu\nu}f^{\mu\nu}
+    +
+    \int d^4\xi\,j^\mu a_\mu
+\end{align}
